<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lighthouse of Alexandria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 280 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Pharos of Alexandria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lighthouse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hvar">Pharos</a>, was a tower built on the island of Pharos at Alexandria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under the first Ptolemaic kings and completed around 280 BCE. Designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sostratus-of-cnidus">Sostratus of Cnidus</a> and probably more than 100 metres high, it was counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</a>. Damaged by earthquakes, it was in ruins by the 14th century, and the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a> was built on its site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="Lighthouse of Alexandria" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prof. Hermann Thiersch (1874–1939) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lighthouse_-_Thiersch.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
